Explain the anatomical structure and function of the mandible in the human body, including its role in the skeletal system and its relationship with other facial bones.
The mandible, or lower jaw, is the largest, strongest, and lowest bone in the human face. It forms the lower jaw and holds the lower teeth in place. The mandible sits beneath the maxilla and is the only movable bone of the skull (excluding the ossicles of the middle ear). 

Anatomically, the mandible consists of the body, which is curved and gives structure to the chin, and two surfaces and two borders. The mandible articulates with the left and right temporal bones at the temporomandibular joints, where the teeth sit in the upper part of the body. 

Functionally, the mandible plays a crucial role in the skeletal system, providing support and structure to the lower face. Its primary function is to hold the lower teeth in place, allowing for proper mastication (chewing) and speech. The mandible's mobility enables the lower jaw to move up and down, side to side, and from front to back, facilitating various facial expressions and oral functions.
