Identify the poet laureate who wrote Idylls of the King, a collection of Arthurian-based poems, and determine their notable works and background in poetry.
The poet laureate who wrote "Idylls of the King" is Alfred, Lord Tennyson. 

Notable works of Alfred, Lord Tennyson include:
- "In Memoriam A.H.H." (a poem written after the death of his close friend Arthur Henry Hallam)
- "The Charge of the Light Brigade" (a poem about the Battle of Balaclava during the Crimean War)
- "Ulysses" (a poem about the character Ulysses from Homer's "The Odyssey")
- "The Lady of Shalott" (a poem based on the Arthurian legend)

Background in poetry:
Alfred, Lord Tennyson was born on August 6, 1809, in Somersby, Lincolnshire, England. He was the fourth of twelve children to Reverend George Clayton Tennyson and Elizabeth Fytche. Tennyson's early education took place at Louth Grammar School and later at Trinity College, Cambridge. He began writing poetry at a young age and published his first collection, "Poems, Chiefly Lyrical," in 1830. Tennyson was appointed as the Poet Laureate of the United Kingdom in 1850, a position he held until his death in 1892.
