Explain the basic principles of sex chromosomes and their role in human genetics
In human genetics, sex chromosomes are a pair of chromosomes that determine the sex of an individual. There are two types of sex chromosomes: X and Y. 

- Females have two X chromosomes (XX), and are called the homogametic sex.
- Males have one X and one Y chromosome (XY), and are called the heterogametic sex.

The X and Y chromosomes play a crucial role in determining the sex of an individual and influencing the expression of certain traits. The X chromosome carries many more genes than the Y chromosome, resulting in more X-linked traits than Y-linked traits. The sex chromosomes also determine the mode of inheritance for certain traits, known as sex-linked inheritance, which differs from the inheritance of traits on autosomal chromosomes.
