Explain how the presence of X and Y chromosomes influences the development of male and female reproductive systems
The presence of X and Y chromosomes influences the development of male and female reproductive systems as follows:

- If the fetus inherits the X chromosome from the father, it will develop into a female. In this case, testosterone is not made, and the Wolffian duct will degrade. The Müllerian duct will develop into female sex organs, and the clitoris is the remnants of the Wolffian duct.

- If the fetus inherits the Y chromosome from the father, it will develop into a male. The presence of testosterone will stimulate the Wolffian duct, which will bring about the development of male sex organs, and the Müllerian duct will degrade.

The presence of the SRY gene on the Y chromosome acts as a signal to set the developmental pathway towards maleness, causing the development of the testes in males and the subsequent release of hormones that cause the paramesonephric ducts to regress. In females, the mesonephric ducts regress.
