What is the historical context of All Saints Day and its origins in Christianity?
The historical context of All Saints' Day and its origins in Christianity can be traced back to the early Christian era. The feast of All Saints, as it is known today, has its roots in the Chaldean Calendar, where a "Commemoratio Confessorum" was celebrated on the Friday after Easter as early as 411. 

In 609 or 610, Pope Boniface IV consecrated the Pantheon in Rome to the Blessed Virgin and all the martyrs, establishing an anniversary feast. This feast was initially celebrated on May 13th, but later Pope Gregory III (731-741) moved the date to November 1st, suppressing the May 13th feast. 

This date coincided with the Celtic holiday of Samhain, which was a harvest festival with themes similar to the Roman festival of Lemuria. The celebration of All Saints' Day was established in the East, and it has since been observed by various Christian denominations, including the Roman Catholic Church, the Anglican Communion, and the Eastern Orthodox Church, among others.
