What is the history of Wimbledon and its major milestones, including the introduction of open-era tennis in 1968?
Wimbledon, the oldest of the Majors, was founded in 1877. Before 1968, only amateurs were allowed to compete in established tennis tournaments, including the four Grand Slams. The "Open Era" began in 1968 when major tournaments agreed to allow professional players to compete with amateurs. This change was made due to the hypocrisy in the sport, specifically the practice of "shamateurism" where amateurs received exorbitant expenses to compete at many tournaments.

The first tournament where professional tennis players were allowed to play at Wimbledon was held in August 1967. The 1968 Wimbledon Championships, held from June 24 to July 6, 1968, marked the start of the Open Era for Wimbledon, becoming the second Grand Slam tournament to offer prize money and allow professionals to compete after the 1968 French Open.
