Describe the design and functionality of the Sam Browne belt, including its original purpose and evolution over time.
The Sam Browne belt is a wide belt, usually made of leather, supported by a narrower strap passing diagonally over the right shoulder (although non-commissioned members wear it over the left shoulder). Its original purpose was to help General Sir Sam Browne, a 19th-century British Indian Army officer who had lost his left arm, to draw his sword more easily by stabilizing the scabbard with the diagonal strap.

Over time, the Sam Browne belt evolved to become a part of military and police uniforms. It has been proposed as a solution to occupational safety and health concerns about injury due to the weight of equipment on police officers' belts. However, its design has also raised concerns about potential physical control of law enforcement officers in an altercation.

The belt's design has remained relatively consistent, with the wide belt supported by a narrower strap passing diagonally over the shoulder. Its functionality has expanded beyond its original purpose, and it has been adopted by various groups, including police officers and some civilians, for both practical and fashionable reasons.
