Explain the historical context and origins of the phrase 'vox populi'.
The phrase 'vox populi' originated in the 8th century, as mentioned in a letter from Alcuin to Charlemagne in 798. Alcuin used the phrase to express concern about the dangers of democratic ideas, stating that "the riotousness of the crowd is always very close to madness." This early reference indicates that the phrase had become an aphorism of political common wisdom by the end of the 8th century.

Over time, the phrase 'vox populi' evolved to take on a more positive connotation, as seen in its use by the Archbishop of Canterbury Walter Reynolds in 1327, who used it to support charges against King Edward II. The phrase gained further popularity in English political history, often being used to express the idea that the voice of the people is a reflection of divine will.

The phrase 'vox populi, vox Dei' (meaning 'the voice of the people is the voice of God') emerged as a variation of 'vox populi', emphasizing the idea that the voice of the people is a divine expression. This phrase was used in various contexts, including a Whig tract of 1709, which expanded on the idea that the voice of the people is a reflection of God's will.
