Investigate the differences between the Eastern Orthodox and Western Christian celebrations of All Saints' Day and how they impact the date of the celebration in the Western Church.
The Eastern Orthodox Church and Western Christian churches have different celebration dates for All Saints' Day. 

The Eastern Orthodox Church celebrates All Saints' Day on the first Sunday after Pentecost. 

In contrast, the Western Christian churches, including the Roman Catholic Church, the Anglican Communion, the Methodist Church, the Lutheran Church, the Reformed Church, and other Protestant churches, celebrate All Saints' Day on 1 November. 

This difference in celebration dates is the primary impact on the date of the celebration in the Western Church, which remains fixed on 1 November every year.
