What are the historical origins of All Saints' Day in the Eastern Orthodox and Western Christian traditions, and how have their dates evolved over time?
The historical origins of All Saints' Day in the Eastern Orthodox and Western Christian traditions are as follows:

In the Western Christian tradition, the date of All Saints' Day is believed to have been chosen to replace the Celtic festival of Samhain, which was celebrated on 1 November. However, it is also suggested that the date was chosen due to its association with the Germanic festival of the dead, rather than a Celtic one. The date of 1 November was first adopted in the 7th/8th century in Ireland, but it was later adopted by the Roman Catholic Church and other Western Christian denominations.

In the Eastern Orthodox tradition, the feast of All Saints is commemorated on the first Sunday after Pentecost, known as All Saints' Sunday (Greek: Αγίων Πάντων, ""Agiōn Pantōn""). This date was adopted from the Byzantine tradition, and it is still observed by the Eastern Orthodox Church and associated Eastern Catholic Churches and Byzantine Lutheran Churches.

Over time, the dates of All Saints' Day have evolved differently in the Eastern and Western Christian traditions. While the Western Christian tradition has maintained the date of 1 November, the Eastern Orthodox tradition has maintained the date of the first Sunday after Pentecost.
