Identify the location of the loop of Henle within the nephron and describe its significance in the concentration of the urine.
The loop of Henle is located between the proximal convoluted tubule and the distal convoluted tubule within the nephron. 

Its main function is to create a concentration gradient in the medulla of the kidney through a countercurrent multiplier system, which uses electrolyte pumps. This process creates an area of high urea concentration deep in the medulla, near the papilla. As a result, the concentration of the urine increases dramatically, particularly in the descending limb of the loop of Henle, where osmolality can reach up to 1400 mOsmol/kg. This is crucial for the generation of a concentrated urine.
