What is the historical context of Arthur Cayley's work in mathematics, specifically his contributions to algebra and matrix theory?
Arthur Cayley's work in mathematics, specifically his contributions to algebra and matrix theory, occurred in the mid-to-late 19th century. He helped found the modern British school of pure mathematics and made significant contributions to the field of matrix theory. 

In 1858, Cayley published his "A memoir on the theory of matrices," in which he proposed and demonstrated the Cayley-Hamilton theorem. This theorem states that every square matrix is a root of its own characteristic polynomial. He also investigated and demonstrated the non-commutative property of matrix multiplication as well as the commutative property of matrix addition.

Cayley's work in matrix theory was revolutionary, as it proposed and demonstrated the concept of matrix operations independent of equation systems. His contributions to algebra and matrix theory laid the foundation for later mathematicians, such as Felix Klein, who used Cayley-Klein metrics to provide a foundation for non-Euclidean geometry.
