The phenomenon you're referring to is called the 'lighthouse effect' and it occurs in rotating neutron stars, specifically pulsars. When a pulsar rotates, it emits a beam of electromagnetic radiation from its magnetic poles. This beam can only be observed when it's pointing towards the Earth, much like a lighthouse beam can only be seen when it's directed towards an observer. As the pulsar rotates, the beam sweeps past the Earth, producing a periodic pulse of radiation that can be detected. This is why pulsars appear to pulse, with the interval between pulses ranging from milliseconds to seconds.
