Explain the historical origins and evolution of the musical technique of 'word painting' (also known as tone painting or text painting), including its development in different musical styles and periods.
The musical technique of 'word painting' (also known as tone painting or text painting) has its historical origins in the Gregorian chant, dating back to at least the time of Gregorian chant. In this style, musical patterns were used to express not only emotive ideas but also theological meanings.

Word painting developed further in the late 16th century among Italian and English composers of madrigals, to such an extent that word painting devices came to be called madrigalisms. This style of word painting was characterized by the use of musical notes to illustrate word meanings, with composers trying to match the natural rhythms and patterns of human speech.

In the context of the provided content, it is mentioned that word painting originated in secular music, but it is also noted that it was used in the Christian faith, particularly in the setting of Biblical Latin texts. For example, the musical pattern FA-MI-SOL-LA was used to signify the humiliation and death of Christ and His resurrection into glory.

Overall, the evolution of word painting involved its development in different musical styles and periods, from the early use of musical patterns in Gregorian chant to its further development in the madrigal style of the late 16th century.
